Fix stale DPD bearer token surviving a sandbox->live switch (v0.113)

DpdAuthService caches the DPD bearer/refresh token pair in memory for up to
24h to avoid re-authenticating on every call. getAccessToken() only ever
checked that cache against its own expiry, never against whether the
environment/API key/secret it was issued under still matched what's
currently configured in Settings > DPD. So switching Settings from sandbox
to live (or just pasting in new live credentials) without restarting the
container left a still-unexpired, sandbox-issued token being sent to the
live API on every call - DPD rejects it, which surfaced as live services
silently not appearing on the order screen despite the credentials and
environment setting both actually being correct.

Added a currentCredentialsFingerprint() (environment + api key + api
secret, joined) recorded alongside the cached token at the moment it's
actually issued (login()/refresh() via applyTokenResponse()).
getAccessToken() now compares the current fingerprint against that before
trusting the cache, and calls resetConnection() to force a fresh login the
moment any of the three change, rather than silently reusing or refreshing
the stale token. The v0.109 "Reset DPD connection" button in Settings still
exists as a manual override for other cases (e.g. DPD support asking for a
fresh token after an account-side change), but shouldn't be needed for a
routine sandbox/live switch any more.

Also tidied up a docblock that had drifted above the wrong method
(resetConnection() instead of getAccessToken()) from the v0.109 change.

